From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Lu=C3=ADs=20Duarte?= <lduarte@cloudflare.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 13:42:31 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Implement HTML directionality and bidi isolation

Every inline layout resolved bidi with parley's whole-text autodetect,
ignoring CSS direction, so dir=rtl blocks rendered with whatever base
the text happened to start with. The inline-layout root now stamps its
computed direction on the tree builder, and unicode-bidi:plaintext (pre
dir=auto) maps to AutoPerParagraph.

Directionality follows whatwg/html#9796, and the dir-shadow WPT suite
pins each edge:

- Node::html_directionality_is_rtl walks the light tree plus the
  shadow-root -> host crossing. Slotted nodes do not take HTML
  directionality from their assigned slot even though CSS direction
  inherits through the flat tree (dir-shadow-03: ltr :dir with rtl
  computed on the same element). :dir() matches against it.
- dir=auto cannot ride a selector: its first-strong scan excludes
  bdi/script/style/textarea subtrees and dir-attributed descendants
  (the dir_auto-contained-* reftests probe exactly this). The scan runs
  in tree order with parley::first_strong_direction per text node,
  defaulting to ltr, and resolves into the cascade as a presentational
  hint on direction, so getComputedStyle and flat-tree inheritance see
  the result and author direction rules override it.
- The scan uses assigned nodes when the auto element is a slot and
  light children otherwise. Slots met as descendants contribute
  nothing, since assigned nodes must not leak into shadow-side scans
  (dir-shadow-30/34), and shadow trees are not descendants of their
  host (dir-shadow-33).

The UBA runs over the whole paragraph text and knows nothing about
markup, so a strong character inside <span dir=rtl> reordered its LTR
siblings; HTML requires dir-attributed inlines (and bdi/bdo) to be
directional isolates. Layout construction wraps each inline element
whose computed unicode-bidi is non-normal in the matching controls:
isolate -> LRI/RLI ... PDI; isolate-override -> isolate + LRO/RLO ...
PDF+PDI; legacy embed/bidi-override -> LRE/RLE/LRO/RLO ... PDF. The
isolate direction is the element's computed direction, not first-strong
of the rendered stream, since a host's shadow text must not steer its
isolate (the dir-shadow-33 reference uses dir=ltr). The controls are
invisible outside the resolver: Cf characters contribute no glyphs
(parley contributes_to_shaping), TextBrush attribution is keyed by node
id, and no offset-based consumer crosses into DOM space. Spans with
unicode-bidi: normal inject nothing.

Also fix bdo: the UA rule 'bdo, bdo:-moz-has-dir-attr' was dropped whole
because one selector fails to parse; bdo[dir] restores the intended
specificity over the [dir] rules.

create_text_editor maps computed direction and unicode-bidi onto the
parley editor, but unicode-bidi:plaintext still resolved to a single
whole-text direction. HTML's dir=auto on textarea, pre and input means
the per-paragraph first-strong heuristic, so map plaintext to
parley::BaseDirection::AutoPerParagraph.

The UA rule that gives those elements plaintext used
:-moz-dir-attr-like-auto, which servo stylo does not support, so it
never matched. Replace it with textarea/pre/input[dir="auto" i].

HTML computes a text control's auto- directionality from its value, but
the scan walked DOM children: inputs have none and a textarea's children
are only the default value, so :dir() and the cascade hint disagreed
with the rendered direction after script value writes (paint was already
right via unicode-bidi:plaintext). Read the editor's value_override
first (set_text_input_value restyles the control on every IDL write, so
no new invalidation is needed), then the value attribute for inputs;
textareas fall through to child text = the default value.

The input type attribute is an ASCII-case-insensitive enumerated
attribute whose unknown/empty values fall to the text state; and three
gates matched lowercase literals only: construct's editor gate, the
pre-layout value seed, and the taffy leaf-measure in layout/mod.rs
(whose text-type list also omitted number, so type="TEXT"/""/"bogus"
rendered as an empty sliver and dropped pre-layout .value writes).
Normalize all three identically; the inline-walk hidden-input checks go
case-insensitive too.
